# Build output
/bin/
/dist/

# The same binaries when they are NOT built through the Makefile. `make build`
# writes into /bin/ above, but a bare `go build ./cmd/zycordd` -- what anybody
# checking one thing quickly actually types -- defaults to the package name in
# the current directory, so it lands here at the root where nothing matched it.
# The desktop section below records that this is not hypothetical: a 16 MB PE
# built that way was committed once already and had to be removed from history
# rather than deleted at the tip. It happened again while this line was being
# written, which is why the pattern is by name rather than a blanket rule --
# a blanket *.exe would also hide packaging/ artefacts somebody may mean to add.
# The list was one name short of complete, and the missing name is `spec/gen`
# -- a `main` package like the other two, so `go build ./spec/gen` lands a
# binary at `/gen`. One was tracked in this repository for a long stretch and
# no text grep ever saw it: a compiled binary embeds the absolute path of every
# source file it was built from, and `git grep -I` skips binaries in silence.
# That is what `sim/wiring.TestNothingPublishedIsOpaqueToTheTextScan` now
# refuses.
/zcd
/zcd.exe
/zycordd
/zycordd.exe
/gen
/gen.exe

# The identity terms for the pre-release anonymity audit: the real name, the
# account handle, the codename of the author's other project. They cannot live
# in a tracked file, because a tracked pattern for a handle publishes the
# handle -- which is the leak it exists to catch.
#
# `sim/wiring` no longer reads this file: the loader and the run-time identity
# class were removed, and RELEASE.md §3 step 2 now points at a terms file kept
# OUTSIDE the repository entirely (`$ZYCORD_IDENTITY_TERMS`). The entry stays
# because the name is the obvious one to reach for, and a terms file created
# here by habit must not become a commit.
/.anonymity-patterns

# Go
*.test
*.out
/coverage.*

# Fuzzing corpora written by `go test -fuzz` (the committed corpus lives in
# testdata/, which is deliberately NOT ignored).
/**/testdata/fuzz/**/[0-9a-f]*

# Editor / OS noise. Never commit these: they leak local paths and usernames,
# and this project is anonymous by design.
.DS_Store
._*
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Desktop wallet build output. Nothing in it is source; /dist/ above already
# covers what `make dist` writes.
#
# The .exe line is not redundant: on Windows the binary must carry the
# extension to be executable at all, so that is the name desktop/README.md and
# the `windows` CI job build, and the extensionless pattern above does not
# match it. Without this an 11 MB binary shows up in `git status` for every
# Windows contributor who follows the README.
#
# "Eventually gets committed" was not hypothetical for long: reviewing the
# branch that added this line, a 16MB PE built by that very README command was
# committed to it, and had to be removed from the history rather than deleted
# at the tip. It was built without -trimpath, so it also carried a local
# username and an absolute path -- the thing the editor/OS section above says
# must never be committed, in a repository that is anonymous by design.
#
# It then happened a third time, and the same sentence explains it: the list was
# one name short of complete. The two names below are the ones desktop/README.md
# tells you to type -- `go build -o zycord-wallet[.exe]`, which also passes
# -trimpath -- and neither is what a bare `go build .` inside desktop/ emits.
# That command names its output after the DIRECTORY, so it writes
# `desktop/desktop[.exe]`, and it passes no -trimpath, so the binary embeds the
# absolute path of every source file it was built from: a local username and a
# home directory, in the one repository that must carry neither. A 7 MB PE of
# exactly that name was committed and merged before anything caught it, because
# no pattern here matched the name and `git grep -I` cannot see inside a binary.
# Keep all four names. The constraint is that the ignore list must cover every
# name the toolchain can choose, not only the names the documentation suggests,
# and `go build` chooses the directory name whenever `-o` is absent.
desktop/zycord-wallet
desktop/zycord-wallet.exe
desktop/desktop
desktop/desktop.exe

# The chaos soak's durable run directory, when ZCD_SOAK_LOGDIR points inside
# the tree. Node logs, per-node data directories and the sampler's TSV land
# here, and the logs carry the absolute path of the directory they were written
# to -- which on a contributor's machine is a home directory and a username, in
# the one repository that must carry neither. The measurements worth keeping
# are extracted into docs/localnet/soaks/ by hand, with the paths stripped;
# the raw tree is never committed.
/.soakrun/
